{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "Under the Consumer Act, any manufacturer, producer or importer is liable for damages caused by a defective product regardless of fault. A product is defective when it fails to provide the safety a consumer rightfully expects, considering its presentation, expected use and the time it entered the market. If the seller does not fix the defect within thirty days, the buyer may choose a replacement, a full refund with monetary updating, or a proportional price reduction. These statutory remedies cannot be waived by a “no‑return, no‑exchange” sign.",
      "evidence": [
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q1" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q2" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s3.q2" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_002",
      "text": "The Consumer Act also sets warranty rules that a buyer should know. An express warranty must be written in clear language, identify the warrantor, state the covered parts, the remedy and the consumer’s duties, and take effect from the moment of sale. To enforce it, the buyer only needs to present the warranty card or receipt to the immediate seller; no other documents are required, and the seller must honor the claim at no cost. If the manufacturer or distributor fails, the retailer is subsidiarily liable. An implied warranty of merchantability lasts at least sixty days but not more than one year. The department can order the seller to repair, replace, or refund the purchase price, and it may not charge the consumer for any of these remedies.",
      "evidence": [
        { "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q2" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q9" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q19" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
