    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "Under Republic Act No. 7394, Art. 97, any manufacturer, producer or importer is liable for damages caused by a defective product regardless of fault. A product is defective when it does not provide the safety that a consumer rightfully expects, taking into account its presentation, the hazards reasonably expected, and the time it was put into circulation. If the defect is not corrected, Art. 100 lets the buyer, after a reasonable period, choose either a replacement of the same kind, an immediate refund with monetary updating, or a proportionate price reduction. The default correction period is thirty (30) days, but the parties may agree to shorten it to no less than seven (7) days or extend it up to one hundred and eighty (180) days.",
      "evidence": [
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q1" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q2" },
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s3.q2" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_002",
      "text": "Art. 68 of the same law provides the basic warranty framework a buyer should read. Any express warranty must be set out in clear, understandable language, identify the warrantor and the party to whom it is extended, state the covered products or parts, describe what the warrantor will do and at whose expense, and tell the consumer what steps to take; it becomes operative from the moment of sale. The retailer is subsidiarily liable if both manufacturer and distributor fail to honor the warranty, and the buyer can enforce the warranty simply by presenting the warranty card or receipt to the immediate seller—no other documents are required. An implied warranty of merchantability lasts at least sixty (60) days but not more than one (1) year for new consumer products.",
